{"item_id": "atlas_test_00003", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "unvulcanized synthetic rubber in soft natural and standard natural forms from New Zealand", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 4002.80.0000):\nThe product in question consists of unvulcanized synthetic rubber imported in sheets, specifically classified as \"soft natural\" and \"standard natural.\" The soft natural rubber is a mixture of natural rubber and styrene-butadiene rubber (SBR), while the standard natural rubber is a mixture of natural rubber, SBR, and acrylonitrile-butadiene rubber (NBA). According to the Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTS), the applicable subheading for synthetic rubber, particularly mixtures of any product of heading 4001 (natural rubber) with any product of heading 4002 (synthetic rubber), is 4002.80.0000. This classification is supported by the ruling issued under Section 177 of the Customs Regulations, which confirms that these products are duty-free under the specified HTS code.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "4002.99.00.00", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 4002 covers synthetic rubber in primary forms or in plates, sheets or strip; chapter 40 note 1 includes synthetic rubber in 'rubber'", "Heading 4001 (natural rubber) excluded because goods are synthetic; heading 4005 applies only if compounded, which is not stated", "GRI 6: polymer type not named among 4002.11-4002.70, not latex, so residual 4002.99 'Other'"], "rationale": "Unvulcanized synthetic rubber in primary forms falls in heading 4002 under GRI 1 and chapter 40 note 1. The 6-digit subheading turns on the polymer type, which the description does not give; 'soft natural' and 'standard natural' read like grade or color names, and a synthetic polyisoprene would go to 4002.60. Absent that fact the residual 4002.99.00.00 is the best guess, consistent with NY I87160 placing an unlisted synthetic rubber in 4002.99.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "4001", "reason": "covers natural rubber; goods described as synthetic rubber"}, {"code": "4005", "reason": "compounded unvulcanized rubber; no compounding (carbon black, silica, other additives) described"}, {"code": "4002.80.00.00", "reason": "mixtures of natural and synthetic rubber; no blend with heading 4001 rubber stated"}, {"code": "4002.60.00.00", "reason": "isoprene rubber (IR) would apply if the polymer is synthetic polyisoprene; not stated"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00008", "reference_source": "ruling:N335170", "product": "a hydrogen gas sensor printed circuit board assembly", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N335170, gold code 9027.10.2000:\nhydrogen gas, which is used to provide concentration level of the sensed hydrogen gas in ppm levels. The sensor is built around a thermal conductivity sensing device that will change voltage dependent on the concentration of thermally conductive hydrogen gas in the environment around the sensor. This voltage is put through a digital to analog conversion, and a corresponding transfer function that provides an analog output voltage. The final sensors will be incorporated into the battery management systems on electric vehicles. As you suggest in your letter, and in accordance with GRI 2(a), the applicable subheading for the printed circuit board assembly for the hydrogen gas sensor will be 9027.10.2000, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS), which provides for “Instruments and apparatus for physical or chemical analysis (for example, polarimeters, refractometers, spectrometers, gas or smoke analysis apparatus); instruments and apparatus for measuring or checking viscosity, porosity, expansion, surface tension or the like; instruments and apparatus for measuring or checking quantities of heat, sound or light (including exposure meters); microtomes; parts and accessories thereof: Gas or smoke analysis apparatus: Electrical.” The general rate of duty will be free. Pursuant to U.S. Note 20 to Subchapter III, Chapter 99, HTSUS, products of China classified under subheading 9027.10.2000, HTSUS, unless specifically excluded, are subject to an additional 25 percent ad valorem rate of duty. At the time of importation, you must report the Chapter 99 subheading, i.e., 9903.88.02, in addition to subheading 9027.10.2000, HTSUS, listed above. The HTSUS is subject to periodic amendment so you should exercise reasonable care in monitoring the status of goods covered by the Note cited above and the applicable Chapter 99 subheading. For background information regarding the trade remedy initiated pursuant to Section 301 of the Trade Act of 1974, you may refer to the relevant parts of the USTR and CBP websites, which are available at https://ustr.gov/issue-areas/enforcement/section-301-investigations/tariff-actions and https://www.cbp.gov/trade/remedies/301-certain-products-china respectively. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided at https://hts.usitc.gov/current. Regarding the country of origin of the PCBA for the hydrogen gas sensor, the manufacturing begins in Germany with the manufacturing of the silicon wafer. The wafer is then shipped to Switzerland where it is diced. In Taiwan, the die is packaged with a ceramic base, bonded to the base with adhesive, and wire bonds are connected from the die to a conductive trace. The packaged die has a laser welded lid with holes for the detection of hydrogen gas. A second version has no holes in the lid and is used as a reference. The devices are then shipped to China, where both thermal conductivity devices are attached to the printed circuit board using surface mount technology (SMT). Additional Chinese components, including the microprocessor, resistors, capacitors, diodes, etc., are also added to the PCB in China using SMT to create a finished PCBA. When the PCBA assembly is imported into the United States, it has the ability to detect and analyze, although the parts are not fully calibrated yet. Therefore, its detection and analyzing capabilities have limited accuracy. However, the", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "9027.10.20.00", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 9027 covers instruments and apparatus for physical or chemical analysis, including gas analysis apparatus", "Section XVI note 1(m) excludes articles of chapter 90 from heading 8543/8534, so the sensor module is not a general electrical article or bare printed circuit", "Chapter 90 note 2(a): a part/module that is itself an instrument of a specific heading stays in that heading; the PCBA performs the gas-detection function itself", "GRI 6: 9027.10 gas or smoke analysis apparatus; 9027.10.20 electrical"], "rationale": "A PCBA that carries a hydrogen sensor and electronics to measure gas concentration is itself an electrical gas analysis apparatus of heading 9027, and Section XVI note 1(m) keeps it out of chapter 85. HQ 967082 classified gas detectors with sensors measuring ppm of gas and displaying levels in 9027.10.20, separating mere alarms into 8531. If the board lacks the sensing element it would instead be a part in 9027.90.88.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "8543.70", "reason": "Section XVI note 1(m) excludes chapter 90 instruments; gas analysis is specifically provided for in 9027"}, {"code": "8531.10", "reason": "only applies to detectors that merely alarm without measuring; description is a sensor measuring hydrogen"}, {"code": "9027.90.88.00", "reason": "parts of gas analysis apparatus; would apply only if the PCBA lacks the sensing element and is merely a control board of a larger analyzer"}, {"code": "8534.00.00", "reason": "bare printed circuits only; this is a populated assembly with a sensing function"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00010", "reference_source": "ruling:N285109", "product": "polyvinyl chloride (PVC) flooring with click-lock installation, consisting of a 4 mm PVC base, a .5 mm PVC backing, and a .5 mm decorative wood veneer layer", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N285109, gold code 3918.10.1000:\ncorative wood veneer layer glued to its surface. You suggest classification in subheading 3916.20.0091, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS) as Monofilament of which any cross-sectional dimension exceeds 1 mm, rods, sticks and profile shapes, whether or not surface-worked but not otherwise worked, of plastics: Of polymers of vinyl chloride: Other. However, as per Explanatory Note 39.16, the subject product is excluded from this heading as it has been further worked (e.g., assembled by gluing) after the extrusion process and is more specifically provided for in heading 3918. The applicable subheading for the flooring will be 3918.10.1000, HTSUS, which provides for Floor coverings of plastics, whether or not self-adhesive, in rolls or in the form of tiles: wall or ceiling coverings of plastic, as defined in note 9 to this chapter: Of polymers of vinyl chloride: Floor coverings: Vinyl tile. The rate of duty will be 5.3 percent ad valorem. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided on World Wide Web at https://hts.usitc.gov/current. This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of the Customs Regulations (19 C.F.R. 177). A copy of the ruling or the control number indicated above should be provided with the entry documents filed at the time this merchandise is imported. If you have any questions regarding the ruling, contact National Import Specialist April Cutuli at april.a.cutuli@cbp.dhs.gov Sincerely, Steven A. Mack Director National Commodity Specialist Division", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "4412.92.52.05", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: candidates 3918 (floor coverings of plastics) and 4412 (plywood, veneered panels and similar laminated wood)", "Explanatory Note to 4412: wood veneered onto a base other than wood (e.g. panels of plastics) is classified in 4412 when the veneer gives the panel its essential character; the 0.5 mm face is a veneer (sheet not over 6 mm, heading 4408)", "Essential character from the wood veneer: it is the visible wearing surface of a wood-faced substitute for wood flooring and the PVC core is hidden when installed (NY N288828)", "GRI 6: 4412.92 other veneered panels with at least one outer ply of nonconiferous wood (assumed non-tropical hardwood); not plywood, so 4412.92.52; statistical suffix 05 wood flooring", "hts_revision_diff: N288828's 4412.99.51.05 was removed in 2022 and the line now sits under 4412.91/4412.92/4412.99 by face species"], "rationale": "The planks are a 0.5 mm wood veneer laminated onto a PVC core; under the EN to heading 4412, wood veneered onto a plastic base is a veneered panel when the veneer gives essential character. NY N288828 (in force) classified an identical 0.5 mm veneer on thermoplastic core flooring in 4412 as wood flooring because the veneer is the visible face and the core is hidden. Its 2017 line 4412.99.51.05 was split in 2022; for an assumed nonconiferous, non-tropical veneer the current line is 4412.92.52.05 (wood flooring).", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "3918.10.10.40", "reason": "PVC floor covering would apply only if the plastic gave the essential character; here the wood veneer face gives it (N288828)"}, {"code": "4412.91.51", "reason": "applies if the face veneer is a tropical wood; species not stated"}, {"code": "4412.99.97.00", "reason": "applies only if the outer plies are coniferous wood"}, {"code": "4412.92.31/4412.92.42", "reason": "plywood provisions; the product is a veneered panel on a plastic core, not plywood"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00013", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "printed motor vehicle fuse cards", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 3926.20.9050):\nThe product in question consists of printed motor vehicle fuse cards made of plastic, measuring approximately 12 inches long by 4 inches wide. These cards are designed to attach to the outside of a fuse box cover and provide information about various fuses in multiple languages. The classification process involves determining the material and function of the product. \n\nThe first card, which has a self-adhesive back, is classified under HTS code 3919.90.5060, which pertains to self-adhesive plates, sheets, film, foil, tape, strip, and other flat shapes of plastics. This classification is appropriate because the card is made of plastic and has a pressure-sensitive adhesive backing, making it suitable for easy application to surfaces.\n\nThe second card, which does not have a self-adhesive back, is classified under HTS code 4911.99.8000, which covers other printed matter. This classification is justified as it is printed material that does not fall under the specified categories of printed matter.\n\nBoth classifications are supported by the material composition (plastic) and the functional characteristics (printed information for vehicle fuses). Therefore, the HTS US code for the printed motor vehicle fuse cards is correctly identified as 3926.20.9050.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "4911.99.80.00", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: printed information chart is the defining feature; Section VII note 2 sends plastics printed with characters that are not merely subsidiary to their use to chapter 49, except goods of heading 3918 or 3919", "Heading 4911 other printed matter; not paper-lithographed matter of 4911.99.60 if printed on plastic", "GRI 6: 4911.99 other, 4911.99.80.00 other", "If the card has a self-adhesive back it stays in 3919 (excepted from Section VII note 2), 3919.90.50.60"], "rationale": "NY F83391 (in force) classified printed motor vehicle fuse cards carrying a fuse chart: the self-adhesive plastic card in 3919.90.50.60 and the non-adhesive card as other printed matter in 4911.99.80. The description does not say whether the cards are self-adhesive, which decides the heading. The best guess is the non-adhesive printed matter line 4911.99.80.00, since Section VII note 2 sends printed plastics other than 3918/3919 goods to chapter 49.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "3919.90.50.60", "reason": "self-adhesive plastic flat shapes; applies only if the card has a pressure-sensitive adhesive back, which is not stated (F83391 used this line for the adhesive version)"}, {"code": "8538.90", "reason": "not a part of switching/protecting apparatus; the card is only an information label"}, {"code": "4911.99.60.00", "reason": "applies only to matter printed on paper by lithography; fuse cards are typically plastic"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00017", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "a steel air conditioning vent", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 7326.90.8587):\nThe product in question is a steel air conditioning vent, which consists of a decorative top and a register used to cover duct openings in homes, controlling the flow of air from heating and cooling systems. The initial proposal for classification under HTS subheading 7322.90.0045, which pertains to parts of air heaters and hot air distributors, was deemed inappropriate because the vents do not serve as parts of the heating or air conditioning machines themselves. Instead, they function as terminations for ducting in HVAC systems. As such, they are classified under heading 7326, which covers \"other articles of iron or steel.\" The specific subheading 7326.90.8587 is applicable as it pertains to other articles of iron or steel that do not fall under more specific categories. The duty rate for this classification is 2.9 percent ad valorem.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "7326.90.86.88", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 8415 parts provision considered; Section XVI note 2 requires a part to be an integral, constituent component of the machine; a room vent is a separate article of the duct system, not a component of the air conditioning machine", "Section XVI note 1(g) / Section XV note: article of steel not otherwise specified falls in heading 7326 other articles of iron or steel", "GRI 6: 7326.90 other; 7326.90.86 other; suffix 88 other"], "rationale": "A steel air conditioning vent is a separate article that fits a duct opening; CBP treats such vents, grilles and return air panels as not integral parts of an air conditioning unit, so heading 8415 does not apply. NY N350010 (in force) classified steel return air vents/access panels in 7326.90.86.88, following earlier vent and grille rulings. The vent falls in the residual steel line 7326.90.86.88.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "8415.90", "reason": "vents are not integral, constituent or component parts of an air conditioning machine (N350010, citing earlier vent and grille rulings)"}, {"code": "8708.29.51.60", "reason": "motor vehicle body parts; applies only to vents for vehicles, not stated"}, {"code": "7308.90", "reason": "not a structure or part of a structure"}, {"code": "3926.90", "reason": "vent is steel, not plastic"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00029", "reference_source": "ruling:R01491", "product": "O-rings used in semiconductor processing chambers", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling R01491, gold code 3926.90.4510:\n Novellus Systems, Inc., you requested a tariff classification ruling. Diagrams and descriptive literature were provided with your letter. The O-rings will be used as vacuum seals with semiconductor manufacturing equipment. O-ring 22-152842-00 is composed of silicone. O-ring 78-2744 is composed of Chemraz, a perfluoroelastomer. O-ring 21-104462-00 is composed of Viton, a fluorocarbon elastomer. The country of origin was not stated in your letter. The rate of duty provided below is the rate applicable for the goods when made in a country with which the United States has Normal Trade Relations. The applicable subheading for the O-rings will be 3926.90.4510, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTS), which provides for other articles of plastics and articles of other materials of heading 3901 to 3914: other: gaskets, washers and other seals…O-rings. The rate of duty will be 3.5 percent ad valorem. This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of the Customs Regulations (19 C.F.R. 177). A copy of the ruling or the control number indicated above should be provided with the entry documents filed at the time this merchandise is imported. If you have any questions regarding the ruling, contact National Import Specialist Joan Mazzola at 646-733-3023. Sincerely, Robert B. Swierupski Director, National Commodity Specialist Division", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "3926.90.45.10", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["Section XVI note 1(g): parts of general use (Section XV note 2) and similar goods of plastics are excluded from Section XVI, so heading 8486 parts do not apply; O-rings are similar to washers of 7318 (parts of general use)", "GRI 1: heading 3926 other articles of plastics", "GRI 6: 3926.90.45 gaskets, washers and other seals; suffix 10 O-rings"], "rationale": "HQ H314335 (in force) affirmed NY N314063, classifying O-rings for semiconductor CVD and HDPCVD processing chambers in 3926.90.45 and rejecting heading 8486 because Section XVI note 1(g) excludes plastic goods similar to parts of general use. The same reasoning applies here, giving 3926.90.45.10 (O-rings). If the O-rings are vulcanized rubber, the parallel line is 4016.93.50.10.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "8486.90.00", "reason": "excluded by Section XVI note 1(g) as a plastic good similar to parts of general use (H314335 affirming NY N314063)"}, {"code": "4016.93.50.10", "reason": "vulcanized rubber O-rings; applies only if the O-rings are of vulcanized rubber rather than plastics (Section XVI note 1(a) would also exclude them from 8486)"}, {"code": "8484.10", "reason": "gaskets of metal sheeting combined with other material; not applicable to a single-material O-ring"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00030", "reference_source": "ruling:N329324", "product": "a women's knee-length coat made of 70% polyester and 30% wool", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N329324, gold code 6102.30.1000:\n357 Ms. Carol-Lynn McGovern Hatley Little Blue House Inc. 4001 Boulevard de la Cote-Vertu Saint-Laurent, QC H4R 1R5 Canada RE: The tariff classification of a women’s coat from China Dear Ms. McGovern: In your letter dated November 14, 2022, you requested a tariff classification ruling. Style F23CIL1675 is a women’s knee-length coat constructed from a 70% polyester, 30% wool knit fabric. The fully lined garment features a full front opening, a shawl collar, long hemmed sleeves, patch pockets, and a hemmed bottom. In addition, the garment includes a wrap closure secured with a self-fabric belt. The applicable subheading for style F23CIL1675 is 6102.30.1000, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS), which provides for Women’s or girls’ overcoats, carcoats, capes, cloaks, anoraks (including ski-jackets), windbreakers, and similar articles, knitted or crocheted, other than those of heading 6104: Of man-made fibers: Other: Containing 23 percent or more by weight of wool or fine animal hair. The rate of duty is 64.4 cents per kilogram plus 18.8 percent ad valorem. Pursuant to U.S. Note 20 to Subchapter III, Chapter 99, HTSUS, products of China classified under subheading 6102.30.1000, HTSUS, unless specifically excluded, are subject to an additional 7.5 percent ad valorem rate of duty. At the time of importation, you must report the Chapter 99 subheading, i.e., 9903.88.15, in addition to subheading 6102.30.1000, HTSUS, listed above. The HTSUS is subject to periodic amendment, so you should exercise reasonable care in monitoring the status of goods covered by the Note cited above and the applicable Chapter 99 subheading. For background information regarding the trade remedy initiated pursuant to Section 301 of the Trade Act of 1974, including information on exclusions and their effective dates, you may refer to the relevant parts of the USTR and CBP websites, which are available at https://ustr.gov/issue-areas/enforcement/section-301-investigations/tariff-actions and https://www.cbp.gov/trade/remedies/301-certain-products-china, respectively. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided on the World Wide Web at https://hts.usitc.gov/current. This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of the Customs Regulations (19 C.F.R. 177). A copy of the ruling or the control number indicated above should be provided with the entry documents filed at the time this merchandise is imported. If you have any questions regarding the ruling, contact National Import Specialist Deanna Boldt via email at deanna.m.boldt@cbp.dhs.gov. Sincerely, Steven A. Mack Director National Commodity Specialist Division", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "6202.40.20.20", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 6202 women's overcoats, carcoats and similar coats, not knitted (assumed woven; knit would be 6102)", "Chapter 62 subheading note / Section XI subheading note: classified by the fiber predominating by weight, polyester (man-made) 70%, so 6202.40 of man-made fibers", "GRI 6: 6202.40 overcoats, carcoats and similar coats > other (not down) > wool content 30% is below the 36% threshold of 6202.40.15, so 6202.40.20 other; not a raincoat, so 'Other: Women's' suffix 20"], "rationale": "A women's knee-length coat is an overcoat or carcoat-type garment of heading 6202 (assuming woven fabric). Polyester predominates by weight, so it falls under 6202.40 of man-made fibers, and 30% wool is below the 36% wool threshold of 6202.40.15, leaving 6202.40.20. Not being a raincoat, the women's statistical line is 6202.40.20.20. No ruling on this exact blend was found.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "6202.40.15.10", "reason": "requires 36% or more by weight of wool or fine animal hair; coat has 30%"}, {"code": "6202.40.20.05", "reason": "raincoats; no water-resistant raincoat feature stated"}, {"code": "6202.20", "reason": "wool does not predominate by weight"}, {"code": "6102.30", "reason": "knitted coats; applies only if the fabric is knitted or crocheted"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00036", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "Dew Point and Temperature Transmitters", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 9025.80.1000):\nThe product in question, Dew Point and Temperature Transmitters, is designed to measure water vapor/moisture content in the air. The classification under HTS code 9025.80.1000 is appropriate because this subheading specifically covers electrical hygrometers, which are devices that measure humidity levels. The ruling indicates that the temperature measurement is secondary and serves to support the primary function of measuring humidity. The accuracy of the devices, as stated, is about one percent for typical conditions, which aligns with the characteristics of electrical hygrometers. The ruling also references Explanatory Note D-2 to Harmonized System Heading 9025, further confirming that these devices fit within the defined scope of this HTS code. Therefore, the classification of 9025.80.1000 is justified for these Dew Point and Temperature Transmitters.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "9025.80.10.00", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 9025 covers thermometers, hygrometers and psychrometers, recording or not, and any combination of these instruments; a dew point meter is a hygrometer and the combined dew point/temperature unit is a combination named in the heading", "Section XVI note 1(m) excludes chapter 90 articles, so 8543 is not available", "GRI 6: 9025.80 other instruments (combinations, not solely thermometers of 9025.1x); electrical, 9025.80.10.00"], "rationale": "Dew point transmitters are electrical hygrometers, and combined with temperature measurement they are a combination of instruments named in heading 9025. Electrical instruments of 9025.80 go to 9025.80.10.00. CBP classified an electronic temperature and humidity monitoring sensor in 9025.80.10 in NY H89862 and recent sensor modules there in NY N355340, both in force.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "9025.19.80", "reason": "thermometers only; the unit also measures dew point (humidity), a combination instrument"}, {"code": "9025.80.35.00", "reason": "non-recording hygrometers that are not electrical; the transmitter is electrical"}, {"code": "9027.80", "reason": "physical or chemical analysis apparatus; humidity and temperature measurement is specifically provided for in 9025"}, {"code": "9026.20", "reason": "pressure measuring instruments; not the measured variable"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00037", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "Vacuum Vessel Housing and Elliptical Collector used in Extreme Ultraviolet (EUV) Scanner for semiconductor lithography", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 8486.90.0000):\nThe Vacuum Vessel Housing and the Elliptical Collector are specifically designed components of an Extreme Ultraviolet (EUV) Scanner, which is utilized in the semiconductor lithography process. The HTSUS code 8486.90.0000 applies to machines and apparatus used principally for the manufacture of semiconductor devices, including parts and accessories. The ruling clarifies that these components are integral to the functioning of the EUV Scanner, which operates by converting molten tin droplets into plasma to emit EUV light for exposing photoresist patterns on silicon wafers. Given that the Vacuum Vessel Housing maintains the necessary vacuum for the light collection and the Elliptical Collector focuses the emitted EUV light, both components are classified under this HTSUS code as they are essential for semiconductor manufacturing processes. The ruling confirms that the applicable tariff classification is correct and that the duty rate for these items is free.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "8486.90.00.00", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: the EUV scanner is a machine of heading 8486 (chapter 84 note 11(D): machines answering to 8486 are classified there and in no other heading)", "Section XVI note 2(b) / heading 8486 text 'parts and accessories': the vacuum vessel housing (first-named article) is not a good of another specific heading and is suitable for use solely with the EUV scanner, so it is a part of 8486", "Section XVI note 1(m) considered: the housing is not an optical element of chapter 90; the elliptical collector mirror is classified separately (possibly 9002.90 as a mounted optical element)", "GRI 6: 8486.90.00.00 parts and accessories"], "rationale": "The first-named article, the vacuum vessel housing, is a dedicated component of an EUV scanner, which is a machine of heading 8486. Under Section XVI note 2(b) and the heading's own 'parts and accessories' text, such a part goes to 8486.90.00.00. CBP has placed dedicated components of ASML EUV systems in 8486.90.00.00 (NY N358520, in force). The elliptical collector mirror may be an optical element of chapter 90 and should be classified separately.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "8486.20.00.00", "reason": "complete lithography machine; the housing is a component, not a machine"}, {"code": "7326.90/7616.99", "reason": "Section XVI note 2(b) sends parts solely for an 8486 machine to 8486.90 rather than general articles of metal"}, {"code": "9002.90", "reason": "mounted optical elements; applies to the elliptical collector mirror if classified separately, not to the vacuum vessel housing"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00038", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "a dynamic image analysis analyzer used for particle size and shape measurement of dry, pourable bulk materials", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 9031.49.9000):\nThe Camsizer is classified under HTS code 9031.49.9000 because it is an optical measuring instrument specifically designed for measuring particle size and shape through dynamic image analysis. The ruling clarifies that the instrument does not perform an analysis of the particles but rather measures their size, distribution, and shape. Previous rulings (HQ 955445 and HQ 088025) have established that similar instruments fall under subheading 9031.49.90, which encompasses other optical measuring or checking instruments. The classification under heading 9027, which pertains to instruments that analyze particles, was deemed incorrect because the Camsizer does not conduct a physical analysis of the particles. Therefore, the appropriate classification for this type of measuring instrument is 9031.49.9000, with a duty rate of 3.5 percent ad valorem.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "9027.50.40.60", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 9027 covers instruments and apparatus for physical or chemical analysis; particle size (granulometric) analysis is physical analysis", "Section XVI note 1(m) excludes chapter 90 instruments from chapter 84/85", "GRI 6: 9027.50 other instruments using optical radiations (visible light imaging); electrical (chapter 90 additional U.S. note 2), 9027.50.40; not chemical, thermal or a photometer, so suffix 60 other"], "rationale": "A dynamic image analyzer determines particle size and shape of bulk powders and granules, which is physical analysis within heading 9027. It works by illuminating particles and capturing images with a camera, so it is an instrument using optical radiation of 9027.50, and being electrical it goes to 9027.50.40. As it is not a chemical or thermal analyzer or photometer, the statistical line is 9027.50.40.60 (other). No ruling on this exact instrument was found.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "9031.49.90", "reason": "optical measuring or checking instruments not elsewhere specified; 9027 more specifically covers apparatus for physical analysis such as particle size analysis"}, {"code": "9027.89", "reason": "other analysis instruments not using optical radiation; the analyzer works by optical imaging"}, {"code": "9027.50.40.15", "reason": "chemical analysis; particle size/shape is physical analysis"}, {"code": "8471", "reason": "not an automatic data processing machine; excluded as a chapter 90 instrument"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00039", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "a shower filter designed to attach between the main plumbing line and the shower head, containing multiple filtration layers to purify water", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 8421.21.0000):\nThe product in question is a shower filter that is specifically designed to purify water by removing impurities and unpleasant odors. Although it is used in the context of a shower, its primary function is as a water filter. The classification suggested by the user, 3922.10.0000, pertains to sanitary ware such as baths, shower baths, sinks, and washbasins, which do not accurately describe the function of the product. The ruling clarifies that the shower filter does not qualify as sanitary ware on its own, thus precluding classification under 3922.10.0000. Instead, the appropriate classification is under 8421.21.0000, which covers filtering or purifying machinery and apparatus for liquids, specifically for filtering or purifying water. This classification aligns with the product's primary purpose and function, confirming that 8421.21.0000 is the correct HTS US code for the shower filter.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "8421.21.00.00", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 8421 covers filtering or purifying machinery and apparatus for liquids; the shower filter is a complete filtering apparatus, not a shower head or plumbing fitting", "Section XVI applies; the article is not a part of a shower head but a stand-alone filter", "GRI 6: 8421.21 for filtering or purifying water, 8421.21.00.00"], "rationale": "An inline shower filter with several filtration layers is apparatus for filtering or purifying water within heading 8421 under GRI 1. It is a complete filter, not a shower head or valve, so subheading 8421.21 (for filtering or purifying water) applies, statistical line 8421.21.00.00. CBP consistently classifies household water filters in 8421.21.0000, for example NY N347101 (in force).", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "8421.99", "reason": "parts of filtering apparatus; the product is a complete filter unit, not a replacement cartridge alone"}, {"code": "3924.90.56/7907", "reason": "shower heads classified by material; the article filters water and is not a shower head"}, {"code": "8481.80", "reason": "taps, valves and similar appliances; the article does not control flow, it filters"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00040", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "a bi-metal hole saw kit including multiple hole saws, mandrels with pilot drills, and a carry case", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 8207.50.2055):\nThe bi-metal hole saw kit (SKU# 93421) consists of multiple components designed for a specific activity, which is drilling. The kit includes six hole saws made of M3 High Speed Steel, two mandrels with attached pilot drills, and a fitted carry case. According to General Rule of Interpretation (GRI) 1, classification is determined based on the terms of the headings and any relevant section or chapter notes. The kit is classified under GRI 3(a) as it contains articles that are prima facie classifiable in different subheadings but are put together for retail sale. Since the hole saws and the mandrels/pilot drills equally impart essential character to the kit, GRI 3(b) does not apply. Therefore, following GRI 3(c), the kit is classified under the heading that occurs last in numerical order among those equally meriting consideration, which is 8207.50.2055. This subheading specifically covers interchangeable tools for hand tools or machine tools, including tools for drilling, which accurately describes the function of the items in the kit. The applicable duty rate is 5 percent ad valorem.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "8202.99.00.00", "deciding_gri": "GRI 3(b)", "gri_path": ["GRI 1 fails: components are prima facie in different headings (hole saws 8202, mandrels 8466.10 per H278181, pilot drills 8207.50, case 4202)", "GRI 3(b) retail set: different articles, put up together for the single activity of cutting holes, packed for retail sale without repacking", "Essential character from the hole saws, which do the cutting and dominate the set's role in use (HQ H097658, NY N355088)", "GRI 5(a): the fitted carrying case is classified with the set", "GRI 6: 8202.99 other saw blades (including parts), 8202.99.00.00"], "rationale": "The kit is a GRI 3(b) retail set of hole saws, mandrels with pilot drills and a fitted case, put up together to cut holes. HQ H097658 classified an essentially identical bi-metal hole saw kit in 8202.99.00 because the hole saws give essential character; H278181 later modified it only as to the separate classification of mandrels (8466.10.01), not the kit. NY N355088 (2025, in force) confirms hole saw sets in 8202.99.0000.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "8466.10.01", "reason": "tool holders; mandrels alone are classified there (H278181), but they do not give the kit its essential character"}, {"code": "8207.50", "reason": "interchangeable drilling tools; the pilot drills only guide the saw and are secondary"}, {"code": "4202", "reason": "the case is a fitted container classified with the set under GRI 5(a)"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00042", "reference_source": "ruling:L85711", "product": "a bib apron made of plastic material with a textile neck strap, piping, and ties", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling L85711, gold code 3926.20.9010:\net while he or she creates “art” by using the other components. You suggest classification in subheading 9505.90.6000 as a costume. However, the item is not a costume, nor a role-play set, nor a toy, but is used by a child while painting or in other creative activity. The plastic apron keeps the other components close at hand and protects clothing. The beret is of insubstantial construction and would likely only be worn in conjunction with the other items. The remaining components will be depleted and replaced. The apron is durable and reusable, and imparts the essential character of the set. The applicable subheading for Lot Number 655-0876 will be 3926.20.9010, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTS), which provides for “Other articles of plastics and articles of other materials of headings 3901 to 3914: Articles of apparel and clothing accessories (including gloves, mittens and mitts): Other: Other, Aprons.” The duty rate will be 5 percent ad valorem. Even though the beret is included as a constituent part of the set for classification purposes, it still falls within textile category 659 and may be subject to textile quota and visa requirements, which are the result of international agreements that are subject to frequent renegotiations and changes. To obtain the most current information as to whether quota and visa requirements apply to this merchandise, we suggest that you check, close to the time of shipment, the “Textile Status Report for Absolute Quotas” available at our web site at www.cbp.gov. In addition, you will find current information on textile import quotas, textile safeguard actions and related issues at the web site of the Office of Textiles and Apparel, at otexa.ita.doc.gov. This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of the Customs Regulations (19 C.F.R. 177). A copy of the ruling or the control number indicated above should be provided with the entry documents filed at the time this merchandise is imported. If you have any questions regarding the ruling, contact National Import Specialist Kenneth Reidlinger at 646-733-3053. Sincerely, Robert B. Swierupski Director, National Commodity Specialist Division", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "3926.20.90.10", "deciding_gri": "GRI 3(b)", "gri_path": ["GRI 1/GRI 2(b): apron is of plastics with textile components, prima facie in 3926.20 (articles of apparel of plastics) and 6211/6307 (textile)", "GRI 3(b) composite: the plastic body forms the protective surface and the bulk of the apron and gives essential character; the textile strap, piping and ties are minor fastening/trim", "GRI 6: 3926.20 articles of apparel; not seamless, gloves or PVC rainwear, so 3926.20.90; statistical suffix 10 aprons"], "rationale": "The bib apron's body is plastic, with only the neck strap, piping and ties of textile. Under GRI 3(b) the plastic body, which supplies the protective function and most of the article, gives essential character, placing it in 3926.20 as an article of apparel of plastics. It is not seamless, a glove or PVC rainwear, so it falls in 3926.20.90, and the statistical suffix for aprons is 10. The textile apron rulings found (e.g. woven polyester aprons in 6211.43) do not apply because the body here is plastic.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "6211.43.00.91", "reason": "woven textile bib aprons; the body here is plastic, textile is only trim and straps"}, {"code": "6307.90.98", "reason": "other made up textile articles; textile does not give essential character"}, {"code": "6210.50", "reason": "garments of textile fabric coated with plastics (heading 5903/5907); the apron is plastic sheeting, not coated textile"}, {"code": "3926.20.90.50", "reason": "'Other' apparel; aprons have their own statistical suffix 10"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00045", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "Interactive Link computer network security products consisting of a Data Diode Device, Keyboard Switch, Multiple Computer Switch, and Data Pump Applications software", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 8471.80.1000):\nThe Interactive Link computer network security products are classified under HTS code 8471.80.1000 because they are considered automatic data processing machines and units thereof, specifically control or adapter units. The ruling explains that the products are packaged together as a set for retail sale, which meets the criteria outlined in General Rule of Interpretation (GRI) 3(b). The Data Diode Device (IL-DDD) is identified as providing the essential character of the set, as it facilitates unidirectional data transmission, ensuring the integrity of the secure network. The Keyboard Switch (IL-KBS) and Multiple Computer Switch (IL-MCS) are passive devices that allow switching between networks without processing data, and the Data Pump Applications (DPA) software is intended for use with these devices. Since the entire set is designed for a specific function related to computer network security, it is classified under the same heading as the Data Diode Device, which is the principal component. The applicable subheading for the Data Diode Device when imported separately is also 8471.80.1000, confirming the classification is consistent across the products. The general rate of duty for this classification is free.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "8517.62.00.90", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["Several different articles named, not a GRI 3(b) set: classify the first-named, the Data Diode Device", "GRI 1: heading 8517 covers other apparatus for the transmission or reception of data in a wired or wireless network; chapter 84 note 6(D) excludes such network transmission apparatus from 8471", "GRI 6: 8517.62.00 machines for the reception, conversion and transmission of data; a data diode passes data one way but is not a modem or switching/routing apparatus, so suffix 90 other"], "rationale": "The description lists several distinct products that do not form a retail set, so the first-named, the Data Diode Device, is classified. A data diode receives and retransmits data between networks in one direction only, which is apparatus for the reception, conversion and transmission of data in heading 8517, subheading 8517.62; chapter 84 note 6(D) keeps it out of 8471. CBP places network data transceivers in 8517.62.0090 (NY N336394, in force); the KVM switches would go to 8471.80.10 separately (NY N332683).", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "8517.62.00.20", "reason": "switching and routing apparatus; the data diode does not switch or route traffic among multiple ports, it only enforces unidirectional transfer"}, {"code": "8471.80.10", "reason": "units of ADP machines (used for KVM switches, N332683); excluded for network data transmission apparatus by chapter 84 note 6(D)"}, {"code": "8543.70", "reason": "8517 more specifically covers network data transmission apparatus"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00047", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "NATCELL and CAR T CELL (Protein)", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 3504.00.5000):\nThe products in question, NATCELL and CAR T CELL, are both protein supplements derived from natural sources. NATCELL is composed exclusively of water and proteins extracted from postnatal veal's thymus gland, while CAR T CELL contains hydrosoluble proteins extracted from the cartilage of the Spinny Dogfish Shark. According to the Harmonized Tariff Schedule, subheading 3504.00.5000 specifically covers \"other protein substances and their derivatives, not elsewhere specified or included.\" Since both products fall under this definition as they are protein supplements derived from animal sources, the classification under HTS code 3504.00.5000 is appropriate. Additionally, the ruling indicates that these products are entitled to a duty rate of 7.9 percent ad valorem, and they qualify for a free rate of duty under the United States-Canada Free Trade Agreement, provided they comply with all applicable regulations.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "3504.00.50.80", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: the products are described only as proteins; heading 3504 covers other protein substances and their derivatives not elsewhere specified", "Chapter 30 considered: 3002 (immunological products, cell therapy products) would apply if the goods are antibodies/cytokine immunological products or living cell therapy products, which the description does not establish", "GRI 6: 3504.00 not a protein isolate, not from peas, so 3504.00.50.80"], "rationale": "The description names two products only as 'Protein', without saying what protein they are or how they are used. If they are protein substances used to support NK or CAR-T cell culture, heading 3504 (other protein substances n.e.s.) applies, as in NY N249832 (laminin protein for cell culture, 3504.00.50, in force), giving 3504.00.50.80. If they are cytokines or antibodies, heading 3002 immunological products would apply, and if they are engineered cells, 3002.51 cell therapy products (NY N325758).", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "3002.51.00.00", "reason": "cell therapy products (e.g. CAR-T therapy YESCARTA, N325758); applies only if the goods are living engineered cells, not a protein"}, {"code": "3002.15/3002.13/3002.14", "reason": "immunological products (antibodies, cytokines, interleukins); applies if the protein is an immunological product, not stated"}, {"code": "3821.00.00", "reason": "prepared culture media; applies only if the goods are a complete culture medium rather than a single protein"}, {"code": "3822", "reason": "diagnostic or laboratory reagents on a backing or prepared kits; not described as such"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00048", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "white reflective polyester film used in the manufacture of LED light panels", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 3920.62.0090):\nThe product in question is identified as white reflective polyester film measuring 0.25 mm in thickness, intended for use in the manufacture of LED light panels. The classification of the film depends on the polymer used to create it. In this case, if the film is made from polyethylene terephthalate (PET), it falls under subheading 3920.62.0090, which covers other plates, sheets, film, foil, and strip of plastics that are noncellular and not reinforced, specifically those made from PET. The ruling clarifies that even if the sheets are used as parts of electrical machines (like LED panels), they are more specifically classified under heading 3920, which pertains to plastic sheets and films. The general rate of duty for this classification is 4.2 percent ad valorem. If the film were made from other types of polyester, the classification would differ, but the specific identification of PET leads to the conclusion that 3920.62.0090 is the correct HTS US code for this product.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "3920.62.00.90", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 3920 covers plates, sheets, film, foil and strip of plastics, noncellular and not reinforced, laminated or supported; film in rolls/sheets is not yet a part of a luminaire", "Chapter 39 note 10: 'plates, sheets, film' covers flat goods in rolls or rectangles, uncut to other shapes", "GRI 6: 3920.62 of poly(ethylene terephthalate); not metallized PET film, so suffix 90 other"], "rationale": "White reflective polyester film is PET film of heading 3920 when it is noncellular, not reinforced or combined with other materials, and imported as flat film. Subheading 3920.62 covers PET, and because the film is white rather than metallized, the statistical line is 3920.62.00.90. NY N326371 (in force) classifies PET film in 3920.62.0090. If the reflector is microcellular or die-cut into a lamp part, 3921.19 or 9405.99 would be considered instead.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "3920.62.00.50", "reason": "metallized PET film; the film is white pigmented, not metallized"}, {"code": "3921.19", "reason": "cellular plastic film; applies only if the white reflector is a microcellular (foamed) PET"}, {"code": "9405.99", "reason": "parts of luminaires; applies only if the film is cut to shape as a dedicated part rather than imported as film material"}, {"code": "3919.90", "reason": "self-adhesive film; no adhesive layer described"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00053", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "a Schwinn 17 function cycle computer", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 9029.20.2000):\nThe product in question is a Schwinn 17 function cycle computer, which is classified under HTSUS Heading 9029. This heading encompasses speedometers and odometers, which are essential functions of the bicycle computer. The device includes an electronic module with digital readouts that display current speed, odometer readings, and additional functions such as trip time and estimated calories burned. The classification is justified as the primary function of the device is to measure speed and distance traveled, which aligns with the description provided in HTSUS 9029.20.2000 specifically for bicycle speedometers. Furthermore, the ruling confirms that the essential character of the product is derived from its speedometer function, thereby solidifying its classification under this HTS code. Additionally, a secondary classification under HTSUS 9902.24.65 applies, which allows for a temporary free rate of duty for bicycle speedometers, confirming the appropriateness of the classification.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "9029.20.20.00", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 9029 covers odometers and speedometers; chapter 90 note 3 applies Section XVI note 3: a multifunction apparatus is classified by its principal function, which is speed and distance indication for a bicycle", "Section XVII note 2(g) excludes chapter 90 instruments from bicycle parts of 8714", "GRI 6: 9029.20 speedometers; 9029.20.20.00 bicycle speedometers"], "rationale": "A 17-function bicycle computer measures and displays speed and distance and adds secondary timing and clock functions. Under chapter 90 note 3 and Section XVI note 3, it is classified by its principal function as a speedometer of heading 9029, and Section XVII note 2(g) keeps it out of bicycle parts in 8714. Subheading 9029.20.20.00 specifically covers bicycle speedometers. No ruling on this exact product was found.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "8714.99", "reason": "Section XVII note 2(g) excludes chapter 90 instruments from bicycle parts"}, {"code": "9029.10.80", "reason": "odometers/revolution counters; speed indication is the principal function and bicycle speedometers are specifically provided for"}, {"code": "9029.20.40", "reason": "other speedometers; bicycle speedometers are named in 9029.20.20"}, {"code": "9106.90/9102", "reason": "clock/stopwatch functions are secondary to the speed and distance measuring function"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00054", "reference_source": "ruling:N343294", "product": "powdered ovalbumin produced via fermentation", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N343294, gold code 3502.11.0000:\ncontrolled environment. In this process, the specific genes responsible for producing ovalbumin are inserted into the microorganisms, which are grown in tanks. As the microorganisms grow, they utilize the genes to produce ovalbumin. After the fermentation process is complete, the ovalbumin is collected, purified, concentrated, and dried into a power; no ingredients are added to the final product. The protein is packaged in various size Kraft paper bags that have a moisture-resistant lining. The powdered ovalbumin can be used as an ingredient in a range of food products under different brands. The applicable subheading for the powdered ovalbumin will be 3502.11.0000, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS), which provides for Albumins (including concentrates of two or more whey proteins, containing by weight more than 80 percent whey proteins, calculated on the dry matter), albuminates and other albumin derivatives: Egg Albumin: Dried. The general rate of duty will be 47.6 cents/kg. If the subject product is of Chinese origin, the following may be applicable to your importations: Pursuant to U.S. Note 20 to Subchapter III, Chapter 99, HTSUS, products of China classified under subheading 3502.11.0000, HTSUS, unless specifically excluded, are subject to the additional 25 percent ad valorem rate of duty. At the time of importation, you must report the Chapter 99 subheading, i.e., 9903.88.03, in addition to subheading 3502.11.0000, HTSUS, listed above. The HTSUS is subject to periodic amendment so you should exercise reasonable care in monitoring the status of goods covered by the Notice cited above and the applicable Chapter 99 subheading. For background information regarding the trade remedy initiated pursuant to Section 301 of the Trade Act of 1974, including information on exclusions and their effective dates, you may refer to the relevant parts of the USTR and CBP websites, which are available at https://ustr.gov/issue-areas/enforcement/section-301-investigations/tariff-actions and https://www.cbp.gov/trade/remedies/301-certain-products-china, respectively. This merchandise may be subject to The Public Health Security and Bioterrorism Preparedness and Response Act of 2002 (The Bioterrorism Act), which is administered by the Food and Drug Administration (FDA). Information concerning FDA import requirements and regulations can be obtained by contacting the FDA at: Food and Drug Administration, Division of Import Operations and Policy, 5600 Fishers Lane, Rockville, Maryland 20857, Telephone: (888) 463-6332. Information on the Bioterrorism Act can be obtained by calling FDA at (301) 575-0156, or at the Web site www.fda.gov/oc/bioterrorism/bioact.html. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided at https://hts.usitc.gov/. The holding set forth above applies only to the specific factual situation and merchandise description as identified in the ruling request. This position is clearly set forth in Title 19, Code of Federal Regulations (CFR), Section 177.9(b)(1). This section states that a ruling letter is issued on the assumption that all of the information furnished in the ruling letter, whether directly, by reference, or by implication, is accurate and complete in every material respect. In the event that the facts are modified in any way, or if the goods do not conform to these facts at time of importation, you should ", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "3502.11.00.00", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 3502 covers albumins, albuminates and other albumin derivatives; ovalbumin is egg albumin by chemical identity regardless of production by fermentation", "Chapter 35 note 1 exclusions (e.g. yeasts of 2102, medicaments of chapter 30) do not apply", "GRI 6: 3502.11 egg albumin, dried (powder), 3502.11.00.00"], "rationale": "Ovalbumin is the principal egg-white protein and is named by heading 3502 as egg albumin; the heading describes the substance, so production by fermentation rather than from eggs should not change the heading. In dried powder form it falls in 3502.11.00.00, the line CBP uses for dried egg-white protein powder (NY N359864, in force). The main risk is that CBP treats non-egg-derived ovalbumin as 'other' albumin in 3502.90.00.00.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "3502.90.00.00", "reason": "other albumins; would apply if CBP treats fermentation-derived ovalbumin as not 'egg albumin' because it is not obtained from eggs"}, {"code": "3502.19.00.00", "reason": "egg albumin not dried; the product is a powder"}, {"code": "3504.00", "reason": "other protein substances n.e.s.; albumins are specifically provided for in 3502"}, {"code": "2106.90", "reason": "food preparations n.e.s.; a single protein is not a food preparation unless compounded with other ingredients"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00055", "reference_source": "ruling:N347842", "product": "an overheight frame attachment from Malaysia", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N347842, gold code 8431.49.1090:\n base that meets the standards of the International Organization for Standardization, it is primarily designed for handling specialized shipping containers (e.g., open top, flat rack, etc.). In your submission, you suggest the overheight frame spreader attachment is classified under subheading 8426.19.0000, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS). We disagree. The overheight frame attachment is not a fixed or stationary machine capable of lifting cargo under its own power, but an attachment that needs to be installed to a crane to accomplish its intended function. As such, the applicable subheading for the overheight frame spreader attachment, model number OSR45, will be 8431.49.1090, HTSUS, which provides for “Parts suitable for use solely or principally with the machinery of headings 8425 to 8430: Of machinery of heading 8426, 8429 or 8430: Other: Of machinery of heading 8426: Other.” The general rate of duty will be free. Effective April 5, 2025, Executive Orders implemented “Reciprocal Tariffs.” All imported merchandise must be reported with either the Chapter 99 provision under which the reciprocal tariff applies or one of the Chapter 99 provisions covering exceptions to the reciprocal tariffs. Products of China, including Hong Kong and Macau, will be assessed an additional ad valorem rate of duty of 125 percent. Products from all other countries will be subject to an additional 10 percent ad valorem rate of duty. Your product falls within an excepted subheading. At the time of entry, you must report the Chapter 99 heading applicable to your product classification, i.e. 9903.01.33, in addition to subheading 8431.49.1090, HTSUS, listed above. On March 12, 2025, Presidential proclamation 10896 imposed additional tariffs on certain derivative iron or steel products. Additional duties for derivative iron or steel products of 25 percent are reflected in Chapter 99, headings 9903.81.89 and 9903.81.90. Products provided by heading 9903.81.91 will be subject to a duty of 25 percent upon the value of the steel content. At the time of entry, you must report the Chapter 99 heading applicable to your product classification, i.e. 9903.81.91, in addition to subheading 8431.49.1090, HTSUS. Derivative iron or steel products processed in another country from steel articles melted and poured in the United States, provided for in heading 9903.81.92, are not subject to the additional ad valorem duties. Please note that derivative steel products admitted to a U.S. foreign trade zone under “privileged foreign status” before March 12, 2025, and entered for consumption on or after March 12, 2025, may be subject to additional duties under heading 9903.81.93, HTSUS. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided at https://hts.usitc.gov/. The holding set forth above applies only to the specific factual situation and merchandise description as identified in the ruling request. This position is clearly set forth in Title 19, Code of Federal Regulations (CFR), Section 177.9(b)(1). This section states that a ruling letter is issued on the assumption that all of the information furnished in the ruling letter, whether directly, by reference, or by implication, is accurate and complete in every material respect. In the event that the facts are modified in any way, or if the goods do not conform to these facts at time of importation, you should br", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "8431.49.10.10", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 8431 covers parts suitable for use solely or principally with machinery of headings 8425 to 8430", "Section XVI note 2(b): the frame is not itself a good of a specific heading and is used principally with container cranes, so it goes with the parts heading for those machines", "GRI 6: 8431.49 (parts of 8426) > 8431.49.10 of machinery of heading 8426 > 8431.49.10.10 of 8426.11/8426.19/8426.30 cranes (assumed gantry/STS container crane)"], "rationale": "An overheight frame is a lifting attachment used with container-handling machinery, and under Section XVI note 2(b) such a dedicated part goes to heading 8431 with the machine it serves. N344880 classified a dedicated crane lifting beam in 8431.49.1010 as a part of 8426 cranes. The statistical line depends on whether the host machine is a gantry/STS crane, a straddle carrier or a forklift, which the description does not state, so the answer abstains.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "8431.20.00.00", "reason": "would apply only if the attachment is for a forklift/top-handler of heading 8427; not stated"}, {"code": "8431.49.10.60", "reason": "would apply if used with straddle carriers or mobile lifting frames rather than gantry/overhead cranes"}, {"code": "8426.19.00", "reason": "the frame is an attachment/part, not a complete crane"}]}}
